<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>

<chapter xml:id="chp-if-switch-statements" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
	<title>Logic, If, &amp; Switch</title>

	<introduction>

		<p>
			So far, we've written code that runs step by step in a straight line. Real programs
			need to react to data or user input, choosing different actions when conditions change.
		</p>

		<p>
			For example, suppose <pf>gift_card_balance</pf> stores the amount of money on an Amazon
			gift card and <pf>total_due</pf> stores the price of a purchase. Before completing the
			purchase, your program needs to ask a yes-or-no question:
			<q>Is <pf>gift_card_balance</pf> greater than or equal to <pf>total_due</pf>?</q>
		</p>

		<p>
			Questions like this are called <term>logical statements</term>, and their answers are
			called <term>logical values</term> (i.e., <pf>true</pf> and <pf>false</pf>). Two types of
			structures that let you ask questions and make decisions based on their answers are
			<pf>if</pf>-statements and <pf>switch</pf>-statements. These structures allow you to
			control the flow of your program, executing different blocks of code depending on the
			conditions you specify.
		</p>

		<p>
			In this section, we will learn how MATLAB evaluates logical statements and how to use <pf>if</pf> and <pf>switch</pf> statements to create programs that can make decisions and respond to different situations.
		</p>
	</introduction>

	<xi:include href="sec-logical-values.ptx" />
	<xi:include href="sec-logical-statements.ptx" />
	<xi:include href="sec-if-statements.ptx" />
	<xi:include href="sec-switch-statements.ptx" />
	<xi:include href="exercises-logic-if-switch-statements.ptx" />

	<conclusion>
		<p>
			<objectives xml:id="takeaways-logic-if-switch-statements">
				<ul>
					<li>
						<term>Logical values are true and false</term>.
						MATLAB has two special values for yes/no answers: <pf>true</pf> and <pf>false</pf>.
						These are of type <pf>logical</pf>, which is different from the numeric type <pf>double</pf>.
					</li>
					<li>
						<term>Single equals vs. double equals</term>.
						A single equals sign (<pf>=</pf>) assigns a value to a variable, while a double equals sign (<pf>==</pf>) compares two values.
					</li>
					<li>
						<p>
							<term>Relational operators compare values</term>.
							The relational operators are:
						</p>
						<program language="matlab">
							<code>
								==   % equal to
								~=   % not equal to
								&lt;    % less than
								&lt;=   % less than or equal to
								&gt;    % greater than
								&gt;=   % greater than or equal to
							</code>
						</program>
					</li>
					<li>
						<term>The NOT operator negates logical values</term>.
						The tilde (<pf>~</pf>) flips <pf>true</pf> to <pf>false</pf> and vice versa. For example, <pf>~(x == y)</pf> is equivalent to <pf>x ~= y</pf>.
					</li>
					<li>
						<p>
							<term>Combining logical statements with AND and OR</term>.
							Use <pf>&amp;</pf> for AND (both must be true) and <pf>|</pf> for OR (at least one must be true).
						</p>
						<program language="matlab">
							<code>
								(age &gt;= 18) &amp; hasLicense  % true only if both are true
								(x &lt; 0) | (x &gt; 100)       % true if either is true
							</code>
						</program>
					</li>
					<li>
						<term>Operator precedence matters</term>.
						When combining logical operators, NOT (<pf>~</pf>) is evaluated first, then AND (<pf>&amp;</pf>), then OR (<pf>|</pf>). Use parentheses to clarify complex expressions.
					</li>
					<li>
						<p>
							<term>if</term>.
							If <pf>condition</pf> is true, run <pf>myCode</pf>:
						</p>
						<program language="matlab">
							<code>
								if condition
									myCode
								end
							</code>
						</program>
					</li>
					<li>
						<p>
							<term>if-else</term>.
							If <pf>condition</pf> is true, run <pf>myCodeA</pf>, otherwise run <pf>myCodeB</pf>.
						</p>
						<program language="matlab">
							<code>
								if condition
									myCodeA
								else
									myCodeB
								end
							</code>
						</program>
					</li>
					<li>
						<p>
							<term>if-elseif-else</term>.
							If <pf>conditionA</pf> is true, run <pf>myCodeA</pf>, otherwise if <pf>conditionB</pf> is true, run <pf>myCodeB</pf>.
							If none are true, run <pf>myCodeC</pf>.
						</p>
						<program language="matlab">
							<code>
								if conditionA
									myCodeA
								elseif conditionB
									myCodeB
								else
									myCodeC
								end
							</code>
						</program>
					</li>
					<li>
						<p>
							<term>switch</term>.
							Compares <pf>value</pf> against a list of exact values.
							If <pf>value</pf> is <pf>valueA</pf>, run <pf>myCodeA</pf>. If <pf>value</pf> is <pf>valueB</pf>, run <pf>myCodeB</pf>.
							If no values match, <pf>myCodeC</pf> runs.
						</p>
						<program language="matlab">
							<code>
								switch value
									case valueA
										myCodeA
									case valueB
										myCodeB
									otherwise
										myCodeC
								end
							</code>
						</program>
					</li>
					<li>
						<p>
							<term>Choosing between <pf>switch</pf> and <pf>if</pf>-<pf>elseif</pf></term>.
							Use <pf>switch</pf> when you are comparing one variable against a small set of exact values (especially strings or integers).
							Use <pf>if</pf>-<pf>elseif</pf> when your conditions involve ranges, inequalities, or multiple variables.
						</p>
					</li>
				</ul>
			</objectives>
		</p>
	</conclusion>

</chapter>
